            <ArticleTitle>Nitrous oxide for pain management of first trimester surgical abortion -- a randomized controlled pilot study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>164-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.contraception.2014.09.013</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0010-7824(14)00729-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The objective was to determine feasibility of a study comparing mean pain scores between women randomized to nitrous oxide/oxygen (NO) versus oxygen+oral analgesics for trimester surgical abortion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pilot randomized controlled trial comparing NO (n=10) versus oxygen+oral analgesics (n=10). Feasibility of subject recruitment, and pain and satisfaction scores on a visual analog scale were evaluated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Fifty-seven percent of eligible women participated. Mean pain scores were similar between groups, and mean satisfaction scores were higher for the NO group (77.5 vs. 46.7, P=.048).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The majority of eligible women agreed to participate in this study evaluating an uncommon pain control intervention.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
